---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT Treadmill Review (2026): Specs, Warranty, Black Friday";
const description =
  "The Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT treadmill checked against Horizon’s listing and three independent reviews: 20 x 60 in. deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, 325 lb, no subscription. Plus how its sale pricing works around Black Friday.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "horizon-7-0-at",
    name: "Horizon 7.0 AT",
    category: "Folding Running Treadmill Without a Subscription",
    badge: "Best Horizon for Most People",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Horizon+7.0+AT+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$934-$1,099 at Horizon (14 Oct 2026)",
    specs: "20 x 60 in. deck · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 325 lb capacity · Bluetooth FTMS",
    pros: [
      "A full 60-inch deck, 12 mph and 15% incline at a sale price around $1,000",
      "Lifetime frame and motor warranty plus three years on parts",
      "No subscription: eight built-in programs, QuickDial controls and Bluetooth FTMS for Zwift, Peloton and Kinomap",
      "The only current Horizon that pairs with Apple Watch (GymKit) and Samsung Galaxy Watch",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Horizon does not publish a motor rating, so there is no official figure to compare",
      "Reviewers report that a heart-rate strap in its monitor-only mode cannot be used alongside a training app",
      "One year of labour against lifetime frame and motor cover",
      "At 277 lb it folds, but it is not a machine you move around casually",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The Horizon to buy unless you need a wider deck or more capacity, and one of the better-specified subscription-free running treadmills near $1,000. Buy it at the sale or code price, never the reference price.",
    description: "The 7.0 AT has a 20 by 60-inch running area on a three-zone cushioned deck, a speed range of 0.5 to 12 mph and motorised incline from 0 to 15% driven by a 500 lb-thrust incline motor, with no decline. Maximum user weight is 325 lb. The console pairs a 7.25-inch LCD with four LED windows, eight programs, QuickDial speed and incline controls, custom interval keys, a fan and Bluetooth speakers, and it sends live metrics over Bluetooth FTMS to apps including Zwift, Peloton and Kinomap. It is the only current Horizon that lists Apple Watch, Apple GymKit and Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing. Assembled it measures 76 by 35 by 67 inches, folds to 44 inches long and weighs 277 lb. The warranty is lifetime on the frame and motor, three years on parts and one year on labour. On 14 October 2026 horizonfitness.com listed it at $1,099 against a $2,100 reference price, or $934 with a promotional code.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Reviews", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/reviews/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Horizon 7.0 AT Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT treadmill good?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On its published figures and three independent reviews, yes, for runners and walkers of average build. It has a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating and lifetime frame and motor cover with three years on parts, and needs no subscription. Horizon does not publish a motor rating. We have not tested it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What motor does the Horizon 7.0 AT have?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Horizon describes a RapidSync drive and a 500 lb-thrust incline motor but publishes no horsepower rating. Reviews quote 3.0 HP or 3.0 CHP, and one says it inferred the figure from earlier versions. The lifetime motor warranty and the fast measured speed changes are more reliable signals than an unofficial number.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will the Horizon 7.0 AT be on sale for Black Friday?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "We cannot predict that. On 14 October 2026 Horizon sold it at $1,099 against a $2,100 reference price, or $934 with a promotional code. Judge any Black Friday offer against that code price and the delivered total, not against the reference price, and compare it with the Bowflex T6 and Horizon T202 on the same day.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Horizon 7.0 AT need a subscription?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Its eight programs, dials and display work on their own. It sends speed, incline and distance over Bluetooth FTMS to apps such as Zwift, Peloton and Kinomap, which have their own fees, but the apps read the data rather than control the machine, so you set speed and incline yourself.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Horizon 7.0 AT vs 7.4 AT: which should I buy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both run to 12 mph and 15% on a 60-inch deck. The 7.4 AT adds a 22-inch belt, a 350 lb rating, an included heart-rate strap, and five years of parts with two of labour, for about $565 more at the code prices we saw. Broad, heavy or daily runners benefit most; others are well served by the 7.0 AT.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Horizon 7.0 AT work with Apple Watch?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Horizon lists Apple Watch pairing through Apple GymKit and Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing, the only current Horizon to do so. Hold the watch near the console and confirm on the watch. Reviewers describe it as easy, with occasional dropouts that reconnect quickly.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-14",
      dateModified: "2026-10-14",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      offers: {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
        availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-14",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-14",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "horizon fitness 7.0 at treadmill",
      "horizon 7.0 at treadmill black friday",
      "horizon 7.0 at review",
      "horizon 7.0 at vs 7.4 at",
      "horizon 7.0 at vs bowflex t6",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Verdict", link: "#who-should-buy" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-horizon70attreadmill" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-horizon70attreadmill)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Model Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmill Review</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The 7.0 AT is the cheapest Horizon with a full 60-inch running deck and the model most Horizon buyers should start from. This review sets out what Horizon and independent reviewers agree on, the one figure Horizon does not publish, what reviewers consistently report, and how to judge its price around Black Friday.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#specifications" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Specifications
          </a>
          <a href="#black-friday" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Black Friday Pricing
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 14, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          14 October 2026 we read Horizon’s US product page for the 7.0 AT (SKU HTM1457-01), including
          its full specification table, warranty, delivery and guarantee terms, and Horizon’s help-centre
          articles on its warranties, programs and electrical requirements. We cross-checked the figures
          against three independent reviews of the current version, updated in November 2024, December
          2024 and April 2026. Horizon and those reviews agree on the 20 x 60 in. running area, 0.5-12 mph,
          0-15% incline, 325 lb maximum user weight, 76 x 35 x 67 in. assembled size, 7.25 in. LCD with
          four LED windows, eight programs and the lifetime frame and motor, 3-year parts and 1-year
          labour warranty; two of them also give the 277 lb weight and the 44 in. folded length. Horizon
          does not publish a motor rating; the reviews quote 3.0 HP or 3.0 CHP, and one says it inferred
          the figure from earlier versions, so we leave it out. We searched cpsc.gov and found no recall
          of the 7.0 AT. We have not tested it, and none of these figures are our own measurements. Prices
          are those shown on horizonfitness.com on 14 October 2026 and change often. We earn a commission
          if you buy through the Amazon link on this page; it does not change what we say about the
          machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT Treadmill: The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#specifications" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Horizon 7.0 AT Specifications, Checked</a>
          <a href="#motor" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The Motor Horizon Does Not Rate</a>
          <a href="#deck-and-incline" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Deck, Cushioning and 15% Incline</a>
          <a href="#console-and-apps" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Console, Apps, Watches and the Bluetooth Catch</a>
          <a href="#owner-reports" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. What Independent Reviews Consistently Report</a>
          <a href="#space-power-delivery" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Folding, Space, Power and Delivery</a>
          <a href="#black-friday" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Horizon 7.0 AT Black Friday and Sale Pricing (as of 14 October 2026)</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. The Warranty, Read Properly</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. 7.0 AT vs 7.4 AT, T202 and Bowflex T6</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Who Should Buy the Horizon 7.0 AT</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT treadmill is the entry to Horizon’s Studio Series, and on paper it
          is a complete running machine: a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, incline to 15%, a 325 lb rating
          and a lifetime warranty on the frame and motor, with a console that needs no subscription and
          sends your data to whichever app you already use. Independent reviewers agree on those
          figures and on what the machine is like to live with. What Horizon does not publish is a
          motor rating, and what it does publish is a $2,100 reference price that it does not sell at.
          This page reads both properly, and ends with a dated look at what a good sale price for the
          7.0 AT looks like.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT treadmill is a well-specified folding running treadmill
            with no subscription, and at its usual sale price of around $1,000 it is the Horizon most
            buyers should start from.</strong> A 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating and
            three years of parts cover on top of a lifetime frame and motor warranty is a strong set of
            published figures at that price.
          </p>
          <p>
            Independent reviewers agree on what it does well: speed and incline change quickly, the motor
            is quiet, the frame stays steady at pace, and pairing an Apple or Samsung watch is easy. They
            also agree on its main irritation, which is Bluetooth: a heart-rate strap paired in the
            treadmill’s monitor-only mode cannot run alongside a training app. The figure to read
            carefully is the one that is missing. Horizon does not publish a motor rating, so the
            "3.0 HP" you will see in reviews and listings is not Horizon’s number.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buy it if you run or walk at pace and are of average build. Step up to the 7.4 AT if you are
            broad or heavy, and look at the T202 or T101 if you only walk. Never pay the reference price;
            the <a href="#black-friday" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday section</a>
            below sets out what a good price looks like. We have not tested the 7.0 AT; what follows is
            what Horizon’s figures and independent coverage show.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
        {
          PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-3">
                  <div>
                    <p class="text-white font-black text-xl leading-tight">
                      {index + 1}. {product.name}
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4 flex-wrap">
                    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400">Typical street price</span>
                    <span class="ml-auto font-black text-gray-900">{product.priceRange}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-400 mb-4">{product.specs}</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6" set:html={product.description} />
                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-2">What Works</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{pro}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">What Doesn't</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{con}</li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-4">
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold mb-4">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                      <a href={product.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
                        Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
                      </a>
                      {product.reviewUrl && (
                        <a href={product.reviewUrl} class="text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline text-sm hover:underline">
                          Read our full {product.name} review &rarr;
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="specifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Horizon 7.0 AT Specifications, Checked</h2>
<p>
            The first part of this table lists figures that appear on Horizon’s current 7.0 AT page and
            in at least one independent review of the current version. The second part lists what only
            Horizon gives, or what conflicts.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Specification</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Running area</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and three reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed / incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.5-12 mph / 0-15%, no decline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and three reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and three reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Assembled size (L x W x H)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">76 x 35 x 67 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and three reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folded size (L x W x H)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">44 x 35 x 68 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and one review</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">277 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and two reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.25 in. LCD + 4 LED windows, 8 programs, no touchscreen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and three reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Watch pairing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Apple Watch (GymKit) and Samsung Galaxy Watch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and two reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime frame and motor; 3 yr parts; 1 yr labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon and two reviews</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price seen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,099 ($2,100 reference); $934 with code</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">horizonfitness.com, 14 October 2026</td></tr>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Horizon only, or conflicting</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Figure</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Status</th>
              </tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor rating</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not published; reviews say 3.0 HP or 3.0 CHP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unconfirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Deck, belt, rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.7 in. deck, 1.6 mm belt, 60/46 mm tapered rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">500 lb thrust</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Step-on height</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8 5/8 in. (Horizon); "just under 9 in." to the belt (one review)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Consistent</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heart-rate strap</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon lists a receiver, not an included strap; one review mentions an included strap</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflict</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things follow from the table. The hardware figures are solid: three independent reviews
            match Horizon’s current dimensions exactly, which suggests the machine has not been revised in
            a way that matters since late 2024. And older 7.0 AT reviews with different weights and
            dimensions — 253 lb and 76.5 by 36 by 59 inches turn up in search results — describe an
            earlier version. Horizon’s help centre refers to the current one as the 7.0AT-04, with serial
            numbers beginning TM1021.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Motor Horizon Does Not Rate</h2>
<p>
            Horizon describes the 7.0 AT’s drive as a RapidSync system and its incline motor as having
            500 lb of thrust, but it does not publish a horsepower or continuous-duty rating for it, or
            for any current Horizon treadmill. The three reviews we read give 3.0 HP or 3.0 CHP. One of
            them says plainly that the motor size is not listed and that it arrived at 3.0 HP from earlier
            versions of the machine. A figure nobody can trace to the manufacturer is not one we print as
            a specification.
          </p>
          <p>
            That matters less than it sounds. A horsepower figure is only comparable between brands when
            both use the same basis, and many do not; Bowflex, for example, footnotes its figures as test
            ratings. The two signals that are available are better. Horizon covers the motor for life,
            which is a direct statement of confidence in it. And two independent reviews timed the 7.0 AT
            going from 1 to 12 mph in about 18 seconds and from 0 to 15% incline in 13 to 16 seconds,
            which is quick for a home treadmill and the reason reviewers single it out for interval work.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains peak, continuous and test ratings.
          </p>
          <p>
            What the published figures imply is a machine built for running at moderate paces, incline
            walking and intervals, for one runner of average build. One reviewer suggests it suits
            roughly 3 to 7 miles of running a day and points heavier, higher-mileage users to the 7.4 AT
            and 7.8 AT, whose capacity ratings and parts warranties are higher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Deck, Cushioning and 15% Incline</h2>
<p>
            The 20 by 60-inch running area is the most important thing the 7.0 AT gets right. Sixty inches
            is the length usually recommended for adults who want to run rather than jog, because a full
            running stride needs room front and back. Twenty inches of width is standard and enough for
            most users; broad runners, and anyone whose gait drifts at speed, get two more inches on the
            7.4 AT. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            covers the arithmetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon’s three-zone Variable Response Cushioning uses different shocks front to back: softer
            where the foot lands, firmer where it pushes off. Two reviewers describe the result as
            comfortable, one calling it softer than most decks at this price and firmer than NordicTrack’s,
            the other saying it could see the different-density shocks under the deck. Neither describes
            it as bouncy. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning guide</a>
            explains what deck cushioning can and cannot do for your joints.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline runs from 0 to 15% with no decline. Fifteen percent is above the common 10 to 12% at
            this price and gives real room for steep incline walking — the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            sits comfortably inside it — and for hill intervals, where the quick incline motor helps.
            If you need to train for long descents, there is no decline here; see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Console, Apps, Watches and the Bluetooth Catch</h2>
<p>
            The console is a 7.25-inch high-contrast LCD with four LED windows underneath, so the key
            numbers stay visible when a tablet covers the screen. There is no touchscreen. QuickDial
            controls on the handlebars roll forward and back for speed and incline, with programmable
            interval keys beneath them and quick-select buttons for jumping to set levels. Horizon lists
            eight programs: 5K, Calorie, Custom, Distance, Fat Burn, Hill Climb, Manual and Target Heart
            Rate. There is a fan, Bluetooth and wired audio, and a USB charging port.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No subscription, open apps</h3>
          <p>
            Nothing on the 7.0 AT needs a membership. It sends live speed, incline and distance over
            Bluetooth FTMS, and Horizon names Peloton, Zwift and Kinomap, plus syncing with Strava and
            other tracking apps. Those apps carry their own fees. Note what FTMS does here: the apps read
            the treadmill’s data but do not control it, so when a Zwift route climbs or a class calls for
            a change, you turn the dial yourself. If automatic control is what keeps you training, a
            NordicTrack or ProForm built around iFIT is designed for that; our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a>
            sets out the trade-off, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            ranks the 7.0 AT for that use.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Watches</h3>
          <p>
            The 7.0 AT is the only current Horizon that lists Apple Watch pairing through Apple GymKit,
            and Samsung Galaxy Watch pairing: hold the watch near the console, confirm on the watch, and
            the treadmill’s speed and distance replace the watch’s own estimate. Two reviewers describe
            this as quick and easy; one noted occasional dropouts that were easy to reconnect.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The heart-rate limitation</h3>
          <p>
            The consistent complaint is Bluetooth heart-rate handling. One reviewer reports that when a
            strap or a Garmin watch is paired in the treadmill’s heart-rate-monitor-only mode, the
            treadmill cannot connect to a training app at the same time; another lists Bluetooth
            heart-rate connectivity as the area most in need of improvement. GymKit pairing with an Apple
            Watch avoids the problem. If you train by heart rate through Zwift or another app, pair the
            strap to the app instead of the treadmill, and check that this works for your devices before
            the return window closes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Independent Reviews Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            We list a point here only when it appears in more than one independent source.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fast speed and incline changes.</strong> Two reviews timed 1 to 12 mph at about 18 seconds and 0 to 15% at 13 to 16 seconds, and all three highlight the QuickDial controls for intervals.</li>
            <li><strong>A quiet motor.</strong> Two reviews describe it as quiet; one measured 48 to 65 dB without a user on the belt, the other compared it to a microwave or clothes dryer.</li>
            <li><strong>A stable frame.</strong> One review reports sprinting without the machine shaking, another describes the frame as stable on an uneven floor, and a third calls the deck solid underfoot.</li>
            <li><strong>Comfortable cushioning.</strong> Two reviews describe the deck as cushioned and comfortable without being bouncy.</li>
            <li><strong>Easy watch pairing, awkward strap pairing.</strong> As described above, in two reviews.</li>
            <li><strong>Good value.</strong> All three describe it as inexpensive for what it offers, at the sale price.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Points made in only one review — that the console looks busy and plastic, that the dials are
            easy to bump while running, that the speakers could be louder — are worth knowing but are
            not patterns. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a>
            explains how to tell one from the other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-power-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding, Space, Power and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            Assembled, the 7.0 AT is 76 inches long, 35 wide and 67 tall, about 18.5 square feet. Folded
            with its one-step hydraulic assist it is 44 inches long and 68 tall, about 10.7 square feet.
            At 277 lb it rolls on transport wheels and one reviewer moved it alone, but plan the delivery
            route: the box is 79.5 by 30 by 13.4 inches and about 332 lb.
          </p>
          <p>
            For ceiling height, Horizon’s help centre says to add 12 inches to the tallest user’s height.
            One reviewer, allowing for the 15-inch rise of the deck at full incline, recommends about 21
            inches. In a basement with beams or ducts, use the larger figure and measure the lowest point
            above where you will stand. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space guide</a>
            covers clearance behind the deck as well.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon specifies a 120 V, 60 Hz supply and says the 7.0 AT is not compatible with GFCI
            outlets; its help centre asks for a dedicated 15 to 20 amp circuit with no extension cord or
            surge protector. Garages and basements often have GFCI outlets, so check before delivery. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">circuit guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            Delivery options at checkout are standard (a two-person crew to the main entrance, porch,
            garage or driveway, in select areas), room of choice, and white glove with full assembly and
            packaging removal, priced by product at checkout. Horizon says orders leave its warehouse
            within days and may then be in transit for up to 10 to 15 days, with weekday delivery
            windows. On 14 October 2026 the 7.0 AT could be added to the basket with no future ship date
            shown. Horizon offers a 30-day guarantee with a full refund; its help centre says returns must
            be authorised, go back in the original packaging, and do not refund shipping and handling, so
            read the policy before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="black-friday" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Horizon 7.0 AT Black Friday and Sale Pricing (as of 14 October 2026)</h2>
<p>
            People search for the Horizon 7.0 AT treadmill around Black Friday because it is frequently
            discounted. We cannot tell you what it will cost in November, and nobody else can either;
            what we can do is set out the pricing pattern we saw on 14 October 2026 and how to judge a
            deal when one appears.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How Horizon prices the 7.0 AT</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The reference price is not the selling price.</strong> Horizon shows $2,100 as the regular price and $1,099 as the sale price. The $1,099 is what it actually charges day to day, so a "50% off" banner measured against $2,100 tells you nothing.</li>
            <li><strong>Codes sit on top.</strong> On 14 October Horizon’s page offered $934 with an autumn promotional code (FALLFIT165), alongside a site-wide banner advertising up to $300 off treadmills. The same code price was showing on 13 October. Other Horizon models carried their own codes on the same day.</li>
            <li><strong>Extras come and go.</strong> Horizon’s pages have carried bundle, free-mat and free-shipping offers at other times. Count them only if you would otherwise pay for the item, and check delivery charges at checkout.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Older deal-tracking threads show earlier versions of the 7.0 AT dipping below $900 with a code,
            but those were different machines at different times, and not a guide to this year.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to judge a Black Friday deal on the 7.0 AT</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Compare with the code price, not the reference price.</strong> The useful benchmark is what you could have paid in October with a code — $934 on 14 October. A Black Friday price is only a real saving if it beats that, after delivery.</li>
            <li><strong>Price it delivered.</strong> Room-of-choice or white-glove delivery is charged at checkout. Compare the total for the delivery option you actually need.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the exact model.</strong> The current SKU is HTM1457-01. Third-party listings sometimes carry older versions with different dimensions and features; the Apple Watch pairing is a quick check that you are looking at the current one.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the ship date.</strong> Horizon shows expected ship dates on its product pages when stock is short. A cheaper price with a ship date weeks away may or may not suit you.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about price changes after purchase.</strong> We did not find a published price-adjustment policy on Horizon’s product page. If you buy before Black Friday and the price drops within your 30-day guarantee period, ask Horizon what it will do, and get the answer in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>Cross-shop the same day.</strong> On 14 October bowflex.com had the Bowflex T6, which lines up closely with the 7.0 AT, at $899, and Horizon’s own T202 was $799 with a code. A deal is only a deal relative to the alternatives on the same day.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill Black Friday guide</a>
            covers how sale events work across brands, and
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the best time of year to buy</a>
            sets out the calendar more broadly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            The 7.0 AT carries a lifetime warranty on the frame and motor, three years on parts and one
            year on labour for home use, as published on Horizon’s product page and in its help-centre
            warranty table. Lifetime motor cover is the answer to the missing horsepower figure, and three
            years of parts is the main thing the 7.0 AT buys over Horizon’s Go Series, which has one.
          </p>
          <p>
            One year of labour is the term to plan around: from year two, a covered part may arrive free
            while the visit to fit it does not. Many parts on a treadmill are within reach of a careful
            owner with the manual, but some jobs are awkward on a 277 lb machine. The 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT
            add five years of parts and two of labour, which is part of their price. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read the split. Horizon’s manuals and help centre also tie the warranty to
            correct use — home use only, the right electrical supply, and the maintenance schedule — so
            keep a simple lubrication log.
          </p>
          <p>
            Safety record: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on
            14 October 2026 and found no recall of the 7.0 AT. Horizon’s only treadmill recall in recent
            years is the 2022 recall of T101-05 units, covered in our
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101 review</a>
            and in <a href="/is-horizon-a-good-treadmill-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our Horizon brand assessment</a>,
            which also covers the parent company’s 2026 reporting penalty.
          </p>
        </div>

