        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy the Horizon 7.0 AT</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want a running treadmill without a subscription.</strong> A 60-inch deck,
            15% incline, fast dial controls and open app support at around $1,000 is the core case, and
            the lifetime frame and motor cover backs it up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you use an Apple or Samsung watch.</strong> It is the only current Horizon
            that pairs with both, which keeps treadmill distance and pace in your watch’s records.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step up to the 7.4 AT</strong> if you are broad, close to 325 lb, or will run most
            days; the wider belt and longer parts and labour cover are the right reasons to pay more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you want classes to change the speed and incline for you,
            need decline, or mainly walk on a tight budget, where the
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">T101</a> or T202 does the
            job for less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it at the sale or code price.</strong> Check the delivered total and the ship date,
            and test the strap-and-app combination you plan to use within the 30-day window.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon Fitness 7.0 AT treadmill good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its published figures and three independent reviews, yes, for runners and walkers of average build. It has a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating and lifetime frame and motor cover with three years on parts, and needs no subscription. Horizon does not publish a motor rating. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the Horizon 7.0 AT have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon describes a RapidSync drive and a 500 lb-thrust incline motor but publishes no horsepower rating. Reviews quote 3.0 HP or 3.0 CHP, and one says it inferred the figure from earlier versions. The lifetime motor warranty and the fast measured speed changes are more reliable signals than an unofficial number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will the Horizon 7.0 AT be on sale for Black Friday?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We cannot predict that. On 14 October 2026 Horizon sold it at $1,099 against a $2,100 reference price, or $934 with a promotional code. Judge any Black Friday offer against that code price and the delivered total, not against the reference price, and compare it with the Bowflex T6 and Horizon T202 on the same day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon 7.0 AT need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Its eight programs, dials and display work on their own. It sends speed, incline and distance over Bluetooth FTMS to apps such as Zwift, Peloton and Kinomap, which have their own fees, but the apps read the data rather than control the machine, so you set speed and incline yourself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Horizon 7.0 AT vs 7.4 AT: which should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both run to 12 mph and 15% on a 60-inch deck. The 7.4 AT adds a 22-inch belt, a 350 lb rating, an included heart-rate strap, and five years of parts with two of labour, for about $565 more at the code prices we saw. Broad, heavy or daily runners benefit most; others are well served by the 7.0 AT.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon 7.0 AT work with Apple Watch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
